<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 8 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">The Great Conquerors</h1><p class="path-tagline">Eight lives that redrew the map, from Cyrus to Napoleon.</p><p class="path-intro">A handful of commanders have bent whole continents to their will, and history has argued about them ever since. This path follows eight of them in order, asking what each conquest cost, what it built, and what outlived the conqueror.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Strategist</a> · <a href="/start">the Chronicler</a></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Encyclopedia · Person · c. 600–530 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Cyrus the Great</span><span class="path-step-why">The founder of the Persian empire, remembered as much for tolerance as for conquest.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Encyclopedia · Person · r. 336–323 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Alexander the Great</span><span class="path-step-why">Master of the ancient world by thirty-two, dead a year later; the empire broke, the Greek century it opened did not.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julius-caesar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Encyclopedia · Person · 100–44 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Julius Caesar</span><span class="path-step-why">Gaul conquered, the Republic broken: the general whose name became a title on three continents.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/attila"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/attila.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Encyclopedia · Person · c. 406–453</span><span class="path-step-name">Attila</span><span class="path-step-why">The Hun who bled two Roman empires and vanished with his realm within a decade.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/genghis-khan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Encyclopedia · Person · c. 1162–1227</span><span class="path-step-name">Genghis Khan</span><span class="path-step-why">From orphan of the steppe to ruler of the largest land empire in history.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/timur"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/timur.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1336–1405</span><span class="path-step-name">Timur</span><span class="path-step-why">The last great steppe conqueror, builder of Samarkand and destroyer of nearly everyone else.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1494–1566</span><span class="path-step-name">Suleiman the Magnificent</span><span class="path-step-why">Lawgiver to the Ottomans and terror of Europe, at the summit of a dynasty's power.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1769–1821</span><span class="path-step-name">Napoleon</span><span class="path-step-why">The artillery officer who crowned himself emperor and forced all Europe to reorganise against him.</span></span></a></li></ol>
